    "body": "The reasoning is indeed to prevent that kind of binding. Being able to say that you can create custom elements (i.e. elements whose lifecycle callbacks run, etc.) whose constructors are HTMLElement, instead of a subclass is ... really weird. If you do `new HTMLElement()` you start getting `<my-el>` elements: why not `<aside>` elements, or `<asdf>` elements, or...? It definitely breaks the mental model of `HTMLElement` as an abstract base class.\r\n\r\nThe goal is that, at least for custom elements, instances of a given element constructor should always have the same local name. You're proposing allowing `HTMLElement` to become a custom element constructor that violates this rule, and moreover is only sometimes custom. Really not great...\r\n\r\nI think you're right the note is not correct in that step 4 will catch the case it exemplifies. We should fix it to talk about the two cases in your OP.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
