    "body": "> why not <aside> elements, or <asdf> elements, or...?\r\n\r\nBecause that's not what you bound them to.\r\n\r\n> It definitely breaks the mental model of HTMLElement as an abstract base class\r\n\r\nI guess the question is why it needs to be abstract.\r\n\r\nNote that this question is a lot more salient for something like HTMLDivElement or HTMLQuoteElement.  Why do I have to define a custom constructor of some sort if I just want to create `<div>`'s by constructor instead of being able to do:\r\n\r\n    customElements.define(\"my-el\", HTMLDivElement, { extends: \"div\" });\r\n\r\nand get `new HTMLDivElement` doing the right thing?\r\n\r\n> The goal is that, at least for custom elements, instances of a given element constructor should always have the same local name.\r\n\r\nInstances defined in terms of instanceof?",
