    "body": "Actually, this is more of an issue with `Reflect.construct`, not `Proxy`. Anyhow, here's an example:\r\n```js\r\nlet constructTestElement = true;\r\nclass TestElement extends HTMLElement {};\r\nconst ElementWithDynamicConstruct = new Proxy(TestElement, {\r\n  construct: function (target, name) {\r\n    return constructTestElement ? Reflect.construct(TestElement, [], ElementWithDynamicConstruct) : Reflect.construct(TestElement, [], HTMLElement);\r\n  },\r\n});\r\ncustomElements.define(\"test-element\", ElementWithDynamicConstruct);\r\nnew ElementWithDynamicConstruct();\r\nconstructTestElement = false;\r\nnew ElementWithDynamicConstruct();\r\n```\r\n\r\nNow that I think about this more, I don't think we need this step because of step 3. If *NewTarget* is a builtin element, then there won't be any *definition* in *registry* with constructor equal to NewTarget. So step 2 is purely redundant. ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
